Left elbow pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Anna Smith is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease, who presents with left elbow pain.

The patient reports that her left elbow pain began approximately 1 week ago. She states that she sits at a desk for work and types and remains in a flexed position all day. After work, it is difficult for her to open and close her left elbow. She reports swelling in the left elbow and is right-handed. She denies numbness or tingling in her hands. She also reports weakness in her left arm, but denies anything significant.

Regarding her epilepsy, the patient states that she has not had any recent seizures. She continues to take Keppra. Her last seizure was 1 year ago.

The patient has been seeing a therapist for depression. She reports that she is doing well with the therapy, but continues to feel a little down and stressed. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations.

Regarding her peptic ulcer disease, she had an endoscopy 1 year ago, which revealed an ulcer. She denies any issues since the procedure.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers or chills.
• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow pain.
• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling. Endorses weakness in the left arm.
• Respiratory: Endorses nasal congestion due to allergies.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow: Erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain with palpation of the left olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable left radial pulse.

RESULTS

• X-ray of the left arm: No evidence of any elbow fracture.

• Endoscopy: Revealed gastritis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Anna Smith is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease, who presents with left elbow pain.

Olecranon bursitis.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with left elbow pain, swelling, and difficulty opening and closing her elbow. The x-ray did not reveal any fractures. She works at a desk and types and remains in a flexed position all day.
• Additional Testing: X-ray of the left arm was reviewed and revealed no evidence of any fractures.
• Medical Treatment: Recommend a pillow for support. Rest. Meloxicam 15 mg, once daily.

Epilepsy.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing well. She has not had any recent seizures.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg, twice daily.

Depression.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that she is doing well with therapy.
• Medical Treatment: Consider medical treatment options. Start Lexapro 10 mg, once daily.

Peptic ulcer disease.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient had an endoscopy 1 year ago, which revealed an ulcer. She is currently taking Protonix.
• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg, once daily before meals.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
