summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is a pleasant and cooperative 86-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. The patient has been followed by me since May of 2020 when he first presented with an 8-10 year history of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with a spino-catheter protocol in June 2020, and he underwent shunt surgery on August 1st, 2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in a ventricular-peritoneal configuration was programmed at a level 2.0 was placed.

The patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. He notes that his walking has improved. He is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance, which he was unable to do prior to surgery. However, he still has intermittent difficulties with bladder control and continues to wear a diaper. He denies any issues with sores from the diaper and any decreased or painful urination. His bladder control did improve after surgery, but has leveled out. He notes that his cognitive function has improved as well. He states that he feels less "lost" when conversing with others now. The patient denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is located. He denies any abdominal pain.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
• Adult hydrocephalus

SOCIAL HISTORY

• Patient is a retired male living in a rural area. He is a retired farmer. He enjoys watching TV and reading books. He lives with his daughter who assists him with daily activities.

FAMILY HISTORY

• Daughter reports that his cognitive function has improved. She states that she feels like she can have a normal conversation with him again.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever or unintentional weight loss. Reports impaired ability to carry out daily functions.
• Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and urgency. Denies any genital sores, decreased or painful output.
• Neurological: Reports walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
• Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

• Blood pressure: 124/80
• Heart rate: 64
• Respirations: 18
• Weight: 174 lbs
• Pain: 0/10

RESULTS

• Short PE: Normal. MMSE: 26/30 on recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration. Fund of knowledge: 25/30 on spelling and 25/30 on calculations. Immediate and delayed recall: 2/3 memory words with cuing and 1/3 with multiple choice. This is a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling. The patient was unable to recall any memory words with cuing without and only 1 with multiple choice at that time. Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28. Cranial nerves II-XII grossly intact. Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor exam shows normal bulk and strength. Coordination is slow from no-finger-to-nose. CT scan from 10/15/2020 of the head shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

• Adult hydrocephalus
• Gait impairment
• Urinary incontinence and urgency
• Cognitive impairment

PLAN

I recommended that we continue to monitor the patient in the office every 3 months. I advised the patient that it is unlikely that his movement speed will improve to the level needed to pass a driver safety evaluation at this point. He could, however, enroll in a driver safety program and continue to work towards passing the evaluation at that point. I also advised that he could continue to see me for cognitive therapy to improve his cognitive function. I recommended that he continue to wear a diaper and take sitz baths as needed to prevent sores. We will review the CT scan results today and leave the shunt setting for another 2-3 months.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
