Perioral dermatitis.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jacqueline Miller is a 35-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for chronic disease management of perioral dermatitis.

The patient reports that her perioral dermatitis is doing much better than before. She states that she has a small amount of residual rash. The patient notes that she started using Doxycycline 100 mg once a day because she believes the pharmacist suggested that if it is getting better, to limit it to once a day. The patient notes that she was taking it twice a day and did notice a lot of improvement. She stopped breastfeeding and asked for the Doxycycline at that time. She denies breastfeeding at this time.

The patient is currently using Sulfacetamide wash twice a day. She denies that it is drying her out. She is also using Metro cream after she washes her face. She denies using any lotions. She is not using Cetaphil cleanser or Neutrogena wipes.

The patient is not currently breastfeeding.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Skin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV-V. Bilateral medial cheeks have a few erythematous papules. There is a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Perioral dermatitis.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing much better than before. There is a small amount of residual rash. I recommend that she continue to use Doxycycline 100 mg twice a day. I also recommend that she use Tacrolimus 0.1% ointment twice a day until the rash is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping. I will rewrite her instructions.
• Additional Testing: None indicated.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that Doxycycline can cause gastrointestinal upset and sun sensitivity. She should use sunscreen and wear a hat when she is outside. I advised her that the Tacrolimus ointment may cause a tingling or "needles and pins" sensation that lasts for a few seconds and then goes away. It is not an allergy and it will not cause her skin to thin.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks. I will cancel the Elidel cream prescription. I will give her a few samples of Vanicream. She should not use any anti-aging or plant-based products.

The patient will continue to use Sulfacetamide wash once a day. She will continue to use Metro cream after she washes her face. She will use Tacrolimus ointment until the rash is gone, then once a day for another week before stopping. She will discontinue the use of Doxycycline after 1 week. She will not start any new products until she sees me again. }}<|eot_id|>
