Bobby Smith is a pleasant 72-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. The patient was referred by Dr. Miller. The patient states that he has been having right upper quadrant pain for several weeks. The pain is located on the right side of his abdomen, underneath his ribs. The pain is described as severe and is associated with nausea. The pain is intermittent, but it is more frequent after meals. The patient states that he has lost 25 pounds over the past 3 months. The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, and he was previously advised to handle this with diet and exercise. The patient states that he has been on a ketogenic diet, and this has improved his symptoms of diabetes and blood sugars. He states that he also has a family history of gallbladder disease, as both of his parents had their gallbladder removed.

The patient states that the pain has been affecting his ability to exercise, but he does not exercise a lot after meals. The patient enjoys photography and hiking.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers.
Gastrointestinal: Endorses right upper quadrant pain, nausea.
Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness.

VITALS

BP: 128/88 mmHg.
RR: 16.
Pulse: 68.
