SpO2: 99% on room air.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No ectopic beats.
Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
GI/GU: Abdomen is soft, non-distended. Positive bowel sounds. There is slight guarding to palpation in the right upper quadrant. Positive Murphy’s sign. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Ultrasound of the abdomen shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. There is mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT

1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
2. Type 2 diabetes mellitus.

PLAN

I discussed the findings of his recent ultrasound with the patient. The patient has multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder, mild thickening of the gallbladder wall, and the common bile duct size is within normal limits. I recommend that we remove the gallbladder. I recommended a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. We will make a few small incisions, and insert a scope with instruments to remove the gallbladder. The procedure will take about 1-2 hours, and the patient will spend the night in the hospital for observation. He will need to avoid high fat foods postoperatively and eat foods high in fiber. I will see him in 1 week for follow-up postoperatively. He will be released to go back out in nature in about a week.

