summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is a 28-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 6 months ago and she is unable to recall a specific event that caused the pain. She describes the pain as a deep achy pain located behind her kneecaps. The patient also reports that she hears a "creaking" in her knees at times. The pain is exacerbated by any activity that involves going from a seated to a standing position, as well as by stairs. The pain improves with rest. The patient has attempted to use ice and Ace bandages in the past, and she occasionally takes ibuprofen or Aleve. She notes that she has decreased her running distance from 5-6 miles per day to less than half a mile due to the pain. She denies any redness or swelling in the knees.

MEDICAL HISTORY

Patient reports she has never broken a bone. She does recall breaking her arm when she was in the 6th grade.

SURGICAL HISTORY

Patient denies any prior surgeries.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient is a runner and enjoys running 5-6 miles per day but has decreased this distance to less than half a mile due to knee pain.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports that she occasionally takes ibuprofen or Aleve.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain and creaking.
Skin: Denies redness or swelling.

VITALS

Vitals are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.
Musculoskeletal: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of the legs bilaterally. The remainder of the muscle strength in the lower extremities is 5/5. Examination of the knees: There is no overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin. There is no effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the bilateral knees shows no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT

Bilateral knee pain, likely patellofemoral pain syndrome.

PLAN

At this point, I have discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient. She will continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed for pain. I am also going to recommend physical therapy where they will show her a number of lower extremity exercises. This will help increase her lower extremity strength, mobility and correct any incorrect running mechanics. All questions were answered.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me soon. }}<|eot_id|>
