summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Low back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is a 26-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The patient states that the pain started approximately 2 weeks ago after lifting a box of books. He denies proper lifting technique. He describes the pain as aching and sharp. The pain initially did not radiate but now radiates down his left leg. He denies any numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. He notes that sitting is usually okay, but he is currently experiencing difficulty with prolonged sitting and standing. He denies any weakness, loss of bladder or bowel control.

The patient states that he has been inconsistent with his exercise routine and has a sedentary job. He denies that the pain has affected his ability to perform his job duties. He has a history of soccer. The patient states that he is an Olympic weightlifter. He denies tobacco use. He is a TATnham Hotspur fan. He denies that he has ever taken any muscle relaxants. He has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol with some relief. He denies any discussion of surgery.

The patient reports that his father has back pain.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, or weakness.
Genitourinary: Denies loss of bladder or bowel control.

VITALS

BP: 130/70.
Respiratory rate: 18.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological
- Orientation: Oriented to person, place, and time.
- Cranial Nerves: Intact.
- Motor: Strength 5/5 bilaterally in the upper and lower extremities.
- Sensory: No decreased sensation.
- Reflexes: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.

Musculoskeletal
- Inspection: No erythema or bruising of the back.
- Palpation: Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness.
- Range of Motion: Full range of motion in the lumbar spine with pain with lumbar flexion and extension.

RESULTS

Mri lumbar spine obtained prior to the visit shows disk desiccation, disk bulge with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Lumbar back pain at L4-L5 disk space due to herniated disk with radiculopathy.

Patient education and counseling was provided regarding his current symptoms. I explained that his symptoms are consistent with a herniated disk. I recommended that we proceed with a lumbar spine injection today. He will receive a combination of a steroid and a strong NSAID, or "NSAID," which will be injected into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy. This should help with the inflammation that is causing his pain. All questions were answered.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will get him scheduled for the procedure today.
