summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Angela Johnson is a pleasant 39-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began when she was running around with her 5-year-old niece, who is learning to ride a bike without training wheels. She states she heard a pop in her knee at the time of the injury and has been limping since. She rates her pain as a 4 out of 10 initially, but it has worsened over time. She has been taking Tylenol and wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She denies taking ibuprofen due to a recent ulcer. She states that Tylenol provides minimal relief and she is unable to bear weight on her left leg.

The patient denies taking ibuprofen due to a recent ulcer.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain and swelling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the left knee: Edema present. Effusion present. Moderate range of motion. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman's test. Negative valgus and varus test.

RESULTS

X-ray of the left knee was obtained and reviewed today. There were no fractures or bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee pain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to her current symptoms. I have explained to the patient that she sustained a left MCL strain when she was running with her niece. I have recommended that we treat her conservatively with a knee immobilizer for one week, ice for 3-4 times a day for 20-30 minutes at a time, and Tramadol 50 mg twice a day for pain. I have also advised her to avoid running for 3 weeks. The patient will follow up with me in 3 weeks to reevaluate her condition.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 3 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
